---
import Base from "../layouts/Base.astro";
import Nav from "../components/Nav.astro";
import Footer from "../components/Footer.astro";
---

<Base
  title="About · Tarmoto"
  description="Why we're building Tarmoto — a desktop-first motorcycle route planner."
>
  <Nav />

  <section class="subpage">
    <div class="subpage-container">
      <div class="eyebrow">
        <span class="eyebrow-num">§</span>
        <span class="eyebrow-rule"></span>
        <span class="stamp">About</span>
      </div>

      <h1 class="subpage-title">
        Why we're building <em style="font-style: italic;">Tarmoto.</em>
      </h1>

      <p class="subpage-lede">
        Most motorcycle route apps start on the phone. That's fine for navigation,
        but painful for planning. Tarmoto starts on a real screen — bigger map,
        better context, more control, fewer surprises once you're already on the road.
      </p>

      <div class="subpage-body">
        <h2>The problem</h2>
        <p>
          A great motorcycle ride is twisty and smooth. The curvy part is easy to
          find — most apps optimise for that. The smooth part is the hard one. You
          plan a 200&nbsp;km loop on a phone, half-tap your way around the map,
          and end up on broken farm tracks because nothing told you the surface
          was wrong.
        </p>

        <h2>What we're building</h2>
        <p>
          A desktop-first planner that knows the difference between
          <strong>twisty</strong> and <strong>broken</strong>. You set the start,
          the end, and the stops; we draft a route biased toward better asphalt
          and let you tune curvature, surface minimum, and avoidances. When the
          route is right, you sync it to a small phone companion that opens
          offline and plays back simple cues while you ride. No turn-by-turn
          competition with your existing GPS. No on-bike logins.
        </p>

        <h2>Where the data comes from</h2>
        <p>
          We estimate road quality where data exists, starting from public
          OpenStreetMap surface and smoothness tags. Beta rider feedback is
          planned to refine confidence over time. Segments without signal are
          marked as <em>unknown</em> — you can choose to avoid them rather than
          guess.
        </p>

        <h2>Who is building it</h2>
        <p>
          A small team operating as <strong>Studio81 Labs, s.r.o.</strong>,
          building Tarmoto in the open. We're inviting riders in small batches
          through the waitlist. We'll say so when something slips.
        </p>

        <p>
          If you want updates, <a href="/#waitlist">join the waitlist</a> — one
          email when there's something to share, no marketing list.
        </p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <Footer />
</Base>
